---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';

const metadata = {
  title: 'Copilot in Plain English | WorkSmart-AI',
  description:
    'Every term you will hear in a Copilot session, explained once, without the marketing. Written for the person using it, not the person configuring it.',
  canonical: '/copilot-glossary/',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <article class="copilot-glossary">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">WorkSmart-AI &middot; Copilot curriculum</p>
      <h1>Copilot in plain English</h1>
      <p class="stand">
        Every term you will hear in a Copilot session, explained once, without the marketing. Written for the person
        using it, not the person configuring it.
      </p>
      <p class="stand sub">
        Search or filter below. The tag on each entry says which part of the picture it belongs to.
      </p>

      <section class="core">
        <h2>If you only learn four</h2>
        <dl>
          <div>
            <dt>Agent</dt><dd>
              <b>Who</b> does the work &mdash; a named Copilot with a job, rules, and a fixed set of places it may read.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Skill</dt><dd><b>What</b> that agent can do &mdash; one named task, described once and reused.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Action</dt><dd>
              <b>What it can reach</b> &mdash; the ability to touch a real system: read a library, send a mail, update a
              list.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Workflow</dt><dd>
              <b>When, and in what order</b> &mdash; steps that fire on a trigger and run without you sitting there.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <div class="controls">
        <label for="q" class="hidden">Search the glossary</label>
        <input id="q" class="search" type="search" placeholder="Search a term, or what it does…" autocomplete="off" />
        <div class="filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter by part of the picture">
          <button type="button" data-cat="all" aria-pressed="true">All</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="licence" aria-pressed="false">Licence</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="workiq" aria-pressed="false">Work IQ</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="surface" aria-pressed="false">Surfaces</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="asking" aria-pressed="false">Asking</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="building" aria-pressed="false">Building</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="trust" aria-pressed="false">Trust</button>
          <button type="button" data-cat="adoption" aria-pressed="false">Adoption</button>
          <span class="count" id="count" aria-live="polite"></span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="list">
        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">A</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Action</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                An agent&rsquo;s ability to touch a real system rather than just talk about it &mdash; read a document
                library, send an email, add a row to a list, create a task. Without actions, an agent can only produce
                text.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Also called</b> a tool, or a connector, depending on which screen you are looking at.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Agent</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                A Copilot you have given a name, a job, a set of rules and a fixed set of sources. Instead of explaining
                the task every time, you explain it once and then talk to the thing you made. Colleagues can use it too.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Not the same as</b> Agent Mode, which is ordinary Copilot working harder inside one document.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="surface">
              <h3>Agent Mode</h3><span class="tag">Surfaces</span>
              <p>
                Copilot taking on a whole multi-step job inside Word, Excel or PowerPoint &mdash; building a document or
                reworking a spreadsheet across several moves &mdash; rather than making one edit and stopping.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Agent Store</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                Where ready-made agents are published &mdash; Microsoft&rsquo;s, your organisation&rsquo;s, and third
                parties&rsquo;. Worth a look before you build anything: somebody has often done it already.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Analyst</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                A specialist agent that works with data. Give it a spreadsheet or a set of figures and it reasons
                through the analysis, writes and runs the code, and shows you its working.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Approval step</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                A point in a workflow where a person has to say yes before anything continues. The safety catch on
                automation, and usually the difference between a workflow you can defend and one you cannot.
              </p>
              <p class="not"><b>Also called</b> human in the loop.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Audit trail</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                The record of what Copilot was asked and what it did, kept by your organisation. It exists, and it
                matters for FOI, data-protection requests and investigations.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Autonomy</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                Whether an agent waits to be asked or acts on its own. Most agents you meet are the first kind. The
                second kind needs an owner, a scope, and someone who checks on it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">C</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Commercial data protection</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                The promise that what you type at work stays inside your organisation&rsquo;s boundary and is not used
                to train the public model. It applies when you are signed in with your work account &mdash; which is the
                whole reason to use the work version rather than a personal one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="workiq">
              <h3>Connector</h3><span class="tag">Work IQ</span>
              <p>
                A bridge that lets Copilot read a system that is not Microsoft&rsquo;s &mdash; a student record system,
                a CRM, a ticketing tool. Someone in IT sets these up; you only notice that Copilot suddenly knows more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Consumption and credits</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Agent runs cost money, metered per run rather than covered by a flat licence. Relevant the moment you
                build something that runs on a schedule rather than when you press a button.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="licence">
              <h3>Copilot Academy</h3><span class="tag">Licence</span>
              <p>
                Guided Copilot training already switched on inside Viva Learning for most Microsoft 365 users. Free,
                already paid for, and usually nobody knows it is there.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="surface">
              <h3>Copilot Actions</h3><span class="tag">Surfaces</span>
              <p>
                Copilot carrying out a task on your device or in the browser on your behalf &mdash; filling a form,
                working through a website &mdash; rather than telling you how to do it.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Not the same as</b> an action in the agent sense, which is a capability you grant an agent you built.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="licence">
              <h3>Copilot Chat</h3><span class="tag">Licence</span>
              <p>
                The free tier, included with a work sign-in. Protected, useful, and grounded in the web &mdash; but it
                cannot see your files, mail or meetings unless you paste them in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="surface">
              <h3>Copilot Notebooks</h3><span class="tag">Surfaces</span>
              <p>
                A place to pin the sources for one project &mdash; a set of documents, pages and notes &mdash; so that
                every question you ask is answered from those, and only those. The fix for &ldquo;it keeps dragging in
                the wrong report&rdquo;.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="surface">
              <h3>Copilot Pages</h3><span class="tag">Surfaces</span>
              <p>
                Turns an answer in a chat into a document you and colleagues can edit together. Use it the moment an
                answer is worth keeping; chat threads are disposable, pages are not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="licence">
              <h3>Copilot Premium</h3><span class="tag">Licence</span>
              <p>
                The top tier. Adds the specialist reasoning agents, the ability to build agents, and higher usage
                limits, on top of everything the standard licence gives you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="surface">
              <h3>Copilot Search</h3><span class="tag">Surfaces</span>
              <p>
                Search across your organisation&rsquo;s content that answers the question rather than handing you twenty
                links to open.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Copilot Studio</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                The place where agents are built, tested and published &mdash; without writing code. This is where a
                repeated prompt becomes something your team can use.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Not the same as</b> Power Automate, which follows fixed rules. Studio builds something that exercises
                judgement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="asking">
              <h3>Custom instructions</h3><span class="tag">Asking</span>
              <p>
                Standing context you set once &mdash; your role, your department, how you like things written &mdash; so
                you stop retyping it in every prompt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Custom-engine agent</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                An agent built from scratch by developers rather than configured on top of Copilot. Worth knowing the
                phrase exists so you can recognise when a conversation has left your territory.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">D</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Declarative agent</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                The ordinary kind &mdash; an agent you make by describing what it should do and which sources it may
                use. Everything a business user builds is one of these.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Deep reasoning</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                Copilot taking longer to think before it answers. Slower and more expensive, and worth it for genuine
                analysis rather than for drafting an email.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>DLP</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Data loss prevention &mdash; the rules your organisation sets about what can leave, and where. Copilot
                obeys them, which is occasionally why an answer stops short.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">G</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="workiq">
              <h3>Grounding</h3><span class="tag">Work IQ</span>
              <p>
                Answering from real sources rather than from memory. A grounded answer can cite where it came from; an
                ungrounded one is the model&rsquo;s best guess. Always check which you were given.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">H</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="asking">
              <h3>Hallucination</h3><span class="tag">Asking</span>
              <p>
                A confident, fluent, wrong answer. It is not a bug being fixed next release &mdash; it is how these
                tools work, and it is why verification is your job rather than Microsoft&rsquo;s.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Human in the loop</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>See <b>Approval step</b>.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">I</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Instructions</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                What you write when you build an agent: its job, its rules, its tone, what it must never do. The single
                biggest lever on whether the agent is any good.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="asking">
              <h3>Iteration</h3><span class="tag">Asking</span>
              <p>
                Steering the answer with follow-up turns instead of retyping the prompt from scratch.
                &ldquo;Shorter.&rdquo; &ldquo;More formal.&rdquo; &ldquo;Now for the student audience.&rdquo; The
                cheapest skill on this list and the most under-used.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">K</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="workiq">
              <h3>Knowledge source</h3><span class="tag">Work IQ</span>
              <p>
                The specific places an agent is allowed to read &mdash; a SharePoint site, a set of documents, a
                connector. Narrowing these is what turns a vague agent into a reliable one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">M</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="workiq">
              <h3>Memory</h3><span class="tag">Work IQ</span>
              <p>
                Copilot holding on to preferences and context across sessions, so it does not start from nothing each
                morning. Check what it has remembered occasionally; it is editable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="workiq">
              <h3>Microsoft Graph</h3><span class="tag">Work IQ</span>
              <p>
                The plumbing underneath Work IQ &mdash; the layer that holds your files, mail, calendar, chats and the
                connections between people. You will hear it named in IT conversations and never need to touch it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="licence">
              <h3>Microsoft 365 Copilot</h3><span class="tag">Licence</span>
              <p>
                The paid seat. This is the one that can see your work &mdash; files, mail, chats, meetings &mdash; and
                that appears inside Word, Excel, Outlook and Teams.
              </p>
              <p class="not"><b>Not the same as</b> Copilot Chat, which is free and cannot reach your work data.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Multi-agent orchestration</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                Agents handing work to other agents. Real, occasionally useful, and firmly a job for whoever owns the
                platform rather than for you on a Tuesday afternoon.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">O</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Oversharing</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Copilot surfacing something that was always technically visible to you but practically buried &mdash; an
                old pay file in a site nobody tidied. Copilot did not break a permission. It found one that was already
                wrong.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">P</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Permissions inheritance</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Copilot can only see what your account can already see &mdash; no more, and no less. The answer to
                &ldquo;can it read my colleague&rsquo;s mail?&rdquo; is no, unless you already could.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="asking">
              <h3>Prompt</h3><span class="tag">Asking</span>
              <p>
                What you ask for. A good one carries five things: the <b>goal</b>, the <b>context</b>, the <b>source</b>
                to use, the <b>format</b> you want back, and the <b>tone</b>. Most disappointing answers are missing
                three of those.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="asking">
              <h3>Prompt Gallery</h3><span class="tag">Asking</span>
              <p>
                A library of prompts inside Copilot &mdash; Microsoft&rsquo;s, your organisation&rsquo;s, and ones you
                save yourself. Start here rather than from a blank box.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">R</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="asking">
              <h3>Referencing</h3><span class="tag">Asking</span>
              <p>
                Pointing Copilot at a specific file, person or meeting inside your prompt, usually by typing a slash or
                an oblique. Turns &ldquo;summarise our policy&rdquo; into a question it can actually answer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Researcher</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                A specialist agent that works through a question over several steps, across your work data and the web,
                and comes back with a sourced report rather than a paragraph.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Responsible use</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Your institution&rsquo;s own rules about what AI may be used for &mdash; marking, admissions, personal
                data, student-facing decisions. These sit above anything Microsoft permits, and they are the ones you
                will be held to.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">S</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="asking">
              <h3>Saved and team prompts</h3><span class="tag">Asking</span>
              <p>
                A prompt you keep, and one your team shares. The step between &ldquo;I worked out a good prompt&rdquo;
                and &ldquo;the department has a good prompt&rdquo;.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Sensitivity label</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                The classification stuck to a document &mdash; public, internal, confidential. Copilot respects it and
                passes it on to anything it produces from that document.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>SharePoint agent</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                An agent created straight from a document library, so it answers only from what is in there. The easiest
                first agent anyone builds, and often the only one a team needs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Skill</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                One named thing an agent can do, written out once: &ldquo;triage an enquiry&rdquo;, &ldquo;draft a reply
                in our house style&rdquo;, &ldquo;flag anything about fees&rdquo;. An agent with three clear skills
                beats an agent with one vague instruction.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>Not the same as</b> a workflow. A skill sits ready to be used; a workflow is an order of events that fires
                on its own.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">T</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="adoption">
              <h3>Tacit knowledge</h3><span class="tag">Adoption</span>
              <p>
                What your team knows but has never written down &mdash; so Copilot cannot read it. The most common
                reason a technically correct answer is still the wrong answer for your institution.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="trust">
              <h3>Tenant</h3><span class="tag">Trust</span>
              <p>
                Your organisation&rsquo;s own walled section of Microsoft&rsquo;s cloud. &ldquo;Inside the tenant&rdquo;
                means inside the boundary your data protection promise covers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Trigger</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                What starts a workflow without you: a time of day, an email arriving, a file being added, a form being
                submitted. Choosing the trigger is most of designing the workflow.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">U</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="adoption">
              <h3>Use case</h3><span class="tag">Adoption</span>
              <p>
                A specific job in your actual week that Copilot could take on. Not &ldquo;improve productivity&rdquo;
                &mdash; &ldquo;turn Monday&rsquo;s three committee papers into a one-page brief before Tuesday&rdquo;.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">V</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="asking">
              <h3>Verification</h3><span class="tag">Asking</span>
              <p>
                Checking the output before you use it. This does not transfer to the tool, the vendor or your IT
                department. If it goes out under your name, it is yours.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lgroup">
          <h2 class="letter">W</h2><div class="entries">
            <div class="entry" data-cat="asking">
              <h3>Work, web, or both</h3><span class="tag">Asking</span>
              <p>
                The toggle that decides whether Copilot answers from your organisation&rsquo;s content, from the open
                internet, or from both. It changes the answer completely, and people forget it is there.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="workiq">
              <h3>Work IQ</h3><span class="tag">Work IQ</span>
              <p>
                The layer that gives Copilot an understanding of your organisation &mdash; your documents, mail,
                meetings, and how people and projects connect. It is the reason Copilot&rsquo;s answer is different from
                a public chatbot&rsquo;s, and the reason it can be wrong in specific, local ways.
              </p>
              <p class="not">
                <b>What it is not:</b> a person reading your email. It is a permissions-bound index your account already
                had access to.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="entry" data-cat="building">
              <h3>Workflow</h3><span class="tag">Building</span>
              <p>
                An ordered set of steps that runs on a trigger, usually without you watching. A step can call an agent,
                wait for a person to approve, and then carry on.
              </p>
              <p class="not"><b>Not the same as</b> an agent. The agent is the worker; the workflow is the rota.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>

      <p class="empty" id="empty">Nothing matches that. Try a shorter word, or clear the filter.</p>

      <p class="foot">
        <strong>A note on names.</strong> Microsoft renames things often. The four core terms &mdash; agent, skill, action,
        workflow &mdash; describe how the thing works and will outlast whatever it is called this quarter. If a name here
        does not match what is on your screen, trust the screen and tell us, and we will update this page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .copilot-glossary {
    --navy: #015887;
    --navy-deep: #013e60;
    --orange: #ff7700;
    --sky: #86bfe2;
    --ground: #f8f8f8;
    --surface: #ffffff;
    --surface-2: #eef4f8;
    --ink: #1b0101;
    --ink-soft: #4d4442;
    --rule: #c9dae5;
    --rule-soft: #e2ecf3;
    --tint: #f2f7fa;
    --focus: #ff7700;
    background: var(--ground);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--aw-font-sans, Ubuntu, 'Segoe UI', system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  :global(.dark) .copilot-glossary {
    --navy: #5cb0dd;
    --navy-deep: #8dcdef;
    --orange: #ff9440;
    --sky: #86bfe2;
    --ground: #0b1920;
    --surface: #12242e;
    --surface-2: #183140;
    --ink: #e9f2f7;
    --ink-soft: #a9bfcc;
    --rule: #2c4b5e;
    --rule-soft: #1d3b4b;
    --tint: #0f212b;
    --focus: #ff9440;
  }
  .wrap {
    max-width: 960px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 48px 24px 96px;
  }
  :focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--focus);
    outline-offset: 2px;
